<p>Anthropic’s September 16, 2026 enterprise session on AWS outlines three ways teams can run Claude in the AWS ecosystem: Claude Platform on AWS, Claude through Amazon Bedrock, and Claude Enterprise through AWS Marketplace. The choice is less about which option is universally better and more about identity, data processing, compliance, isolation, administration and how much control the engineering team wants.</p>
<figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/claude-aws-deployment-options-2026.svg" width="1200" height="630" alt="Three Claude deployment paths on AWS: Claude Platform, Amazon Bedrock and AWS Marketplace"></figure>
<h2>The three AWS paths</h2><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Best fit to evaluate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Claude Platform on AWS</td><td>Teams wanting the full Anthropic API with AWS billing and sign-in</td></tr><tr><td>Amazon Bedrock</td><td>Teams that want AWS to remain the sole data processor and need AWS-native controls</td></tr><tr><td>Claude Enterprise in Marketplace</td><td>Organizations wanting the full product with SSO, admin controls and native integrations</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Claude Platform on AWS</h2><p>Anthropic describes this route as access to the full Claude Platform API while AWS handles billing and sign-in. It can make sense for developer teams that want Anthropic’s API experience while keeping procurement and identity inside an AWS relationship.</p>
<h2>Claude through Amazon Bedrock</h2><p>Bedrock places Claude inside AWS’s managed model service. Anthropic’s September session highlights a key difference: AWS remains the sole data processor. That can matter for organizations with strict data-residency, VPC or compliance requirements. The right fit still depends on the exact AWS controls and regional availability required by the workload.</p>
<h2>Claude Enterprise through AWS Marketplace</h2><p>The Marketplace route is aimed at organizations that want Claude Enterprise rather than only an API. Anthropic describes SSO, admin controls and native integrations, with seat and usage billing through AWS Marketplace. This can be useful when central IT wants a managed rollout across teams.</p>
<h2>How to choose between them</h2><p>Start with the workload, not the product name. If developers are building an application, compare API features, latency, model availability and observability. If data isolation is the main requirement, map the exact data path and AWS boundary. If many employees need Claude for knowledge work, compare identity, administration, connectors, spend controls and rollout needs.</p>
<h2>Questions security teams should ask</h2><p>Document what data enters Claude, which systems the model can reach, where prompts and outputs are processed, how access is logged, how administrators can restrict tools, and how offboarding works. Agentic workflows need extra attention because a model that can call tools can create external side effects that a normal chat session cannot.</p>
<h2>Test the same workload on the chosen path</h2><p>Before committing to an architecture, run representative workloads through the candidate setup. Compare output quality, latency, cost, access controls, logging and failure handling. A deployment choice that looks simple on paper can create hidden work if it does not fit your existing identity or data architecture.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is Bedrock the only way to use Claude on AWS?</h3><p>No. Anthropic describes Claude Platform on AWS, Claude on Amazon Bedrock and Claude Enterprise through AWS Marketplace as different paths.</p><h3>Which option should every enterprise use?</h3><p>There is no single answer. The right path depends on data controls, identity, compliance, application architecture and administration needs.</p>
